<Article
  eyebrow="Homelab · Guide"
  title="Reach your whole home network with Tailscale"
  description="A subnet router takes about twenty minutes, opens no new ports, and is the single highest-value thing I have done to my home network."
  date="September 2026"
  readingTime="9 min read"
>
  <p>For a long time my home network had exactly one door. I had opened a port on
  my router so I could get to my photo server from outside the house, and
  everything else lived behind that wall, invisible. If I was away and my ad
  blocker started misbehaving, or I wanted to check on a backup, my options were
  to wait until I got home or to open another port and feel bad about it.</p>

  <p>The thing that fixed it is a <a href="https://tailscale.com">Tailscale</a>
  subnet router. It took about twenty minutes, it opened no new ports, and it is
  the first thing I would tell anyone with a home server to set up. I have no
  affiliation with them. I am just annoyed that nobody told me sooner.</p>

  <SubnetRouterDiagram />

  <h2>What this gets you</h2>

  <p>Every device on your home network becomes reachable from your phone or laptop
  anywhere in the world, by the ordinary address it already has. The bookmark you
  have for your router's admin page keeps working. So does the one for your
  server, your printer, the thing you set up eighteen months ago and forgot the
  name of.</p>

  <p>You open no additional ports. Your router keeps forwarding exactly what it
  forwarded before. From the internet's point of view nothing about your house
  has changed.</p>

  <h2>What you need</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>One machine that is always on.</strong> A home server, a
    Raspberry Pi, a NAS. It needs to be on whenever you want remote access, which
    is the whole job.</li>
    <li><strong>A <a href="https://tailscale.com/pricing">Tailscale</a> account.</strong>
    The personal plan is free and covers far more devices than a house needs.
    Alternatives exist, including self-hosted <a href="https://headscale.net">Headscale</a>
    if you would rather not depend on a company at all. I use Tailscale because
    it took ten minutes and I have not thought about it since, which is the
    highest compliment I can pay a piece of infrastructure.</li>
    <li><strong>Admin access to that machine's shell.</strong> A few commands,
    nothing exotic.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>You do not need to touch your router, and you should not.</p>

  <h2>How Tailscale works, briefly</h2>

  <p>Tailscale is a mesh VPN built on <a href="https://www.wireguard.com">WireGuard</a>.
  It builds a small private network, a "tailnet," across all your devices. Each
  one gets its own address on that network and they talk to each other directly
  and encrypted, regardless of what network they happen to be sitting on. There
  is no central server your traffic flows through, and no port forwarding
  involved in any of it.</p>

  <p>By itself that only connects devices that are running Tailscale. A
  <a href="https://tailscale.com/kb/1019/subnets"><strong>subnet router</strong></a>
  is one of those devices volunteering to pass traffic through to everything else
  on its local network. Your phone asks for an address on your home network, your
  tailnet says "the always-on box back home can handle that," and the traffic
  goes there.</p>

  <p>That single feature is the whole guide. Everything below is how to turn it on
  without hurting yourself.</p>

  <h2>The steps</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Pick the machine carefully</h3>
      <p>This should be something that stays powered on and where you install
      software through an official package. I nearly put mine on the router
      itself, which would have been tidier, until I read that it needed community
      scripts that firmware updates overwrite. Putting the thing your remote
      access depends on somewhere it gets silently wiped is a bad trade for
      tidiness.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Install Tailscale there, and on your phone</h3>
      <p><a href="https://tailscale.com/download">Download</a> it for both and
      sign in with the same account. At this point the two devices can already
      reach each other over the tailnet. Your home network still cannot be
      reached, which is the gap the next steps close.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Find your local network's address range</h3>
      <p>Look at your router's admin page for something like
      <code>192.168.1.0/24</code> or <code>192.168.0.0/24</code>. The number in
      the third position is the part that varies. If your devices have addresses
      like <code>192.168.1.x</code>, yours is <code>192.168.1.0/24</code>.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Advertise the route</h3>
      <p>On the always-on machine, with your own range in place of the example:</p>
      <pre><code>sudo tailscale set --advertise-routes=192.168.1.0/24</code></pre>
      <p>Some systems also need packet forwarding turned on. If your distribution
      does not do this for you, the Tailscale docs give the two <code>sysctl</code>
      lines for your platform.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Approve it in the admin console</h3>
      <p>Advertising a route is a request. Open the Tailscale admin console, find
      the machine, and approve the subnet route. Nothing happens until you do,
      and this is the step I spent fifteen minutes not doing while wondering why
      it was broken.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Turn it on where you want it</h3>
      <p>On your phone and laptop, enable the setting that accepts routes from
      other devices. On mobile it is usually on by default. On a desktop it
      usually is not.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The parts that will bite you</h2>

  <Callout title="Use tailscale set, never tailscale up" icon="⚠" hue="building">
    <p>Both commands appear in every tutorial and they behave differently.
    <code>up</code> re-evaluates your entire preference set and quietly resets
    anything you did not name in that command. If you have configured anything
    non-default, and you probably have, <code>up</code> will undo it and tell you
    nothing.</p>
    <p><code>set</code> changes only what you name. Use it. To undo the route
    later, <code>sudo tailscale set --advertise-routes=</code> with nothing after
    the equals sign. The full
    <a href="https://tailscale.com/kb/1080/cli">CLI reference</a> is worth ten
    minutes of your time before you start.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h3>A subnet router exposes your entire network to everyone on it</h3>

  <p>This is the part I want you to sit with for a second, because it is easy to
  set up and easy to under-think. You have not opened your network to the
  internet. You have opened it to every device signed into your Tailscale
  account.</p>

  <p>That is completely fine while every device is yours. The moment you invite a
  family member or a friend so they can use one service, they get the whole local
  network unless you write an
  <a href="https://tailscale.com/kb/1018/acls">access control rule</a> first. Write
  the rules before you send the invitation. Tailscale's default policy is
  "everyone can reach everything," which is the right default for one person and
  the wrong one the moment there are two.</p>

  <h3>The exit node is a separate thing, and you probably want it off</h3>

  <p>Tailscale also offers an
  <a href="https://tailscale.com/kb/1103/exit-nodes">exit node</a> feature that
  routes all of your internet traffic through your home connection. It sounds
  appealing on hotel wifi. Remember that your home upload
  speed becomes your download ceiling everywhere in the world. Mine is about
  30 Mbps up, so turning this on would cap my phone at 30 Mbps in a coffee shop
  with gigabit fibre. I advertise it and leave it switched off.</p>

  <h3>Your remote traffic will appear to come from one machine</h3>

  <p>By default the subnet router rewrites the source address of forwarded
  traffic, so everything you do remotely looks like it came from that one box. If
  you run local DNS filtering and enjoy the per-device statistics, this will
  flatten them. There is a fix involving a static route on your router and an
  extra flag. I have not bothered, because I like the per-device stats less than
  I dislike touching routing tables.</p>

  <h2>How to check it actually worked</h2>

  <Callout title="Do not trust the admin console alone" icon="◆" hue="homelab">
    <p>I have been burned by this in a different context. I once had a password
    configured on a public service, saw it listed in the admin panel, and believed
    it for sixteen days. The setting was never applied and the site was answering
    anyone who asked.</p>
    <p>A panel showing a setting is a claim that it is configured. Read the
    machine's own state, then test from outside.</p>
  </Callout>

  <p>Three checks, in order:</p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Read the state on the machine</h3>
      <pre><code>tailscale status --json</code></pre>
      <p>Look for your range under the advertised and primary routes. If it is
      advertised but not primary, you skipped the approval step.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Test with wifi genuinely off</h3>
      <p>Turn wifi off on your phone. Not "disconnect from the network," off. I
      have twice congratulated myself on a working remote setup while still on my
      own wifi, which proves nothing at all.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Open something that was unreachable before</h3>
      <p>Your router's admin page is the best test, because it was definitely not
      reachable from outside before you started. If that loads over cellular, you
      are done.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where this does not help</h2>

  <p>Anything you want to share with people who will not install software and
  make an account stays on the public internet. Photo albums shared with family,
  anything with a mobile app that expects a normal address, anything a neighbour
  might open once. Those still need a real public hostname and real
  authentication.</p>

  <p>What this fixes is the administrative half: the dashboards, the settings
  pages, the backup consoles, the things only you ever touch. Those had no
  business being reachable from the internet, and now they are reachable only by
  you.</p>
</Article>
